Question:
Which medication demonstrates the greatest efficacy by not only achieving the lowest average patient blood pressure but also exhibiting the smallest variability in blood pressure readings among patients, indicating consistent performance?

Table Data:
{"Medication": ["Med A", "Med B", "Med C", "Med D", "Med E", "Med F"], "PatientBloodPressure": [[120, 122, 118, 121, 119], [130, 128, 131, 129, 132], [115, 117, 116, 114, 118], [140, 142, 139, 141, 143], [125, 123, 126, 124, 127], [110, 112, 109, 111, 113]]}

User Instruction:
Shrink the bubble markers to small, solid dots to prevent them from overlapping and ensure Med E no longer touches the top border.

Base Code:
{
  "$schema": "https://vega.github.io/schema/vega-lite/v5.json",
  "data": {
    "values": [
      {"Medication": "Med A", "AverageBP": 120, "StdDevBP": 1.58},
      {"Medication": "Med B", "AverageBP": 130, "StdDevBP": 1.58},
      {"Medication": "Med C", "AverageBP": 116, "StdDevBP": 1.58},
      {"Medication": "Med D", "AverageBP": 141, "StdDevBP": 1.58},
      {"Medication": "Med E", "AverageBP": 125, "StdDevBP": 1.58},
      {"Medication": "Med F", "AverageBP": 111, "StdDevBP": 1.58}
    ]
  },
  "title": "Medication Efficacy: Average and Variability of Blood Pressure",
  "layer": [
    {
      "mark": "bar",
      "encoding": {
        "x": {"field": "Medication", "type": "nominal", "axis": {"labelAngle": 0}},
        "y": {"field": "AverageBP", "type": "quantitative", "title": "Average Blood Pressure"},
        "color": {"value": "#4C78A8"}
      }
    },
    {
      "mark": {
        "type": "errorbar",
        "extent": "stdev",
        "ticks": true
      },
      "encoding": {
        "x": {"field": "Medication", "type": "nominal"},
        "y": {"field": "AverageBP", "type": "quantitative"},
